  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/d/d6/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_55.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Giant-Man and the Wasp are watching clips of their previous encounter with the Human Top (see [LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-14]-51) in front of their fan club.&nbsp; They ponder how difficult it was to capture him and express gratitude that he is currently behind bars.\n\nWhat they cannot know is that, miles away, the Human Top is using his spinning powers to escape from prison!&nbsp; After re-donning his uniform, he needs money, so he heads for a bank.&nbsp; Hank and Jan see this reported on the news and immediately spring into action.&nbsp; But they arrive too late to prevent the heist.\n\nBut while Giant-Man and the Wasp are heading home, as fate would have it, the Human Top happens to be heading in the same direction and sees them.&nbsp; When they get home (not realizing they've been followed), they have a normal spat, this time being overheard by the Top outside the window, who realizes how heart-warming it is \"to see two people who are deeply in love that way!\"\n\nHe then swoops in and grabs the belt off of Giant-Man, where he keeps his growth capsules!&nbsp; The Top has found them, and he immediately takes one of the growth capsules and becomes a giant whirlwind.&nbsp; Hank is hurled into a closet, which the Top locks him inside of, and he uses his speed to capture the Wasp inside a small jar.&nbsp; He warns Giant-Man to lay off, takes the Wasp as insurance against his further meddling, and heads out.\n\nBut Giant-Man still has his cybernetic helmet, which he uses to summon ants, who ultimately bring him a shrinking capsule that allows him to slip under the door.&nbsp; In moments, he's back in action as Giant-Man, and since the Human Top is still giant-sized himself, it's easy for Giant-Man to track him down.&nbsp; The two battle briefly before Giant-Man instructs some nearby termites to weaken the roof they're on, and the Top crashes through.\n\nWhile the Top is unconscious, the Wasp pops a shrinking pill into his mouth, and he's recaptured, just as he swears to both of them that the \"next time'll be <i>different</i>!\"\n\nThe newspapers cover the event, and Hank points out Wasp's name in print.&nbsp; To her, that's all very <i>nice</i>, but she thinks she would \"rather see it on a <i>wedding license</i>, you big, blind, wonderful goof!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "The Human Top spins so fast that logic struggles to keep up.\nAgainst all odds, the story remains more passable than painful.",
  "subtitle": "Spinning Its Wheels",
  "review_text": "The story that starts off in a Giant-Man fan club gathering (in Hank's <i>lab</i> no less) with home movies showing highlights from their previous cases... <i>well</i>... that didn't get things started on the right foot.&nbsp; I think the whole fan club is silly to <i>begin</i> with, and seeing some of its members wearing \"Giant-Man Fan Club\" shirts or pins doesn't help!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(How goofy!)]\n\nOn page 2, the Wasp remarks about how \"handsome\" the Human Top is (while reviewing the footage of his capture).&nbsp; This is because, in the <i>film</i>, he removed his helmet and was photographed.&nbsp; But that was [SPAN: severity=warning; text=never shown] in last October's [LINK:1963:TTA_51_giant0|TTA #51|2027-02-09] issue.&nbsp; Its final panel only has the Human Top being led away by police -- with his helmet still on.\n\n<i>Yes</i>, it's true that the scene shown in the footage was shot from the reverse angle, so we can likely assume that his helmet <i>was</i> removed seconds later, thus allowing his actual features to be captured on film.&nbsp; We should also acknowledge the possibility that Jan could also have learned of his true identity during the trial, just before his eventual incarceration.\n\nObviously, none of that matters in the end, as page 18 of <i>this</i> issue shows him unmasked again just before he's sent back to prison.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: I only call this out because, given the later stories we read involving Whirlwind (e.g. 1975's Avengers #139), I was under the assumption that Jan did not recognize him, since he was able to pose as her chauffer.&nbsp; I guess we'll just have to keep reading to see how he accomplished this...]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | PlotDevice | width=24%] While the fan club concept continues to stretch believability, it is <i>nothing</i> when compared to the Human Top's escape from prison on page 3, where Stan has him \"spinning so fast that he is virtually invisible.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=That's just absurd.]\n[BR]\n[BR] Even if you accept the <i>visual </i>claim, any guard passing by <i>surely</i> would have <b>felt</b> something.&nbsp; After all, there was a guy spinning around inside the cell!&nbsp; A police officer even asks (on the next page), \"Where'd that <i>wind</i> come from?\"\n\nThat same page later describes his spinning action as \"causing a miniature tornado,\" so [SPAN: severity=warning; text=please don't try to convince me that the prison guard didn't feel anything, because I'm not <i>buying</i> it!]\n\nBut of course, the Human Top had already been tagged to be this month's villain, so the story needed a way to get him out of prison.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=This is about the worst case of a '<b>Hold your nose and move on'</b> plot device that I've seen recently.]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=The reader is left with no choice but to turn the page with a sinking feeling that this month's adventure might already be doomed.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe last panel of page 5 was unfortunately drawn.\n[ASSET: center | UnfortunatelyDrawn | width=large]\n\nWhen I first looked at it, it appeared that Giant-Man had grown so large as to have to carefully step around cars on the street, and I thought he'd grown taller than ever before, particularly when the Wasp says \"Wait!&nbsp; Stop!&nbsp; You -- you'll <i>kill</i> yourself, Hank!\" (which I thought referred to him growing too tall), but it turns out he was actually [SPAN: severity=warning; text=throwing himself out the open window many stories up] with the intent to grab hold of the tension cable he attached to a reinforced flag pole below.\n\nStan and his artists are so desperate to position Ant/Giant-Man as some sort of brilliant hero that they never stop to consider the most obvious objection:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=A giant-sized man has just hurled himself out of an upper window, planning to grab a hoop extending from a flagpole below.]\n[ASSET: center | WhatIfHeMisses | width=large]\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=What if he misses?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Is there a plan B?)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Sigh...]\n\nIn many ways, this problem goes all the way back to my comments about Ant-Man's very first appearance in [LINK:1962:TTA_35_ant0|TTA #35], where I postulated that Stan and company were so determined to position Hank Pym as some sort of clever genius, that I wrote:&nbsp; [SPAN: fonttype=italic; text=he feels a bit more \"pre-fab\" than some of the other heroes competing for readers' spare change, as if reverse engineered from gadgets and not a character who grows into his role.]\n\nThat was over a year-and-a-half ago, even <i>before</i> his funky catapult was ever depicted -- which wasn't until the <i>next </i>issue.\n\nAnd here we go again...\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Need I mention that the futile race to the bank consumes over three pages of story?&nbsp; We're not <i>getting</i> anywhere here!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | HeartWarming | width=10%] About the only purpose their trip to the bank serves from a plot perspective is to put them in a position where the villain can follow them back to their lab, where he overhears their conversation (including Jan calling Hank by name - [SPAN: severity=warning; text=so much for any secret identity!]).\n\nIt's so obvious how Hank feels about Jan, that even a <i>few moments</i> of quiet eavesdropping on the part of the Human Top allows him to grasp what we readers have known for some time:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=this is no longer a strictly professional partnership.]&nbsp; (Of course, the Wasp herself has never made any attempt to hide her amorous feelings toward Hank.&nbsp; It's just taking Hank a little longer to reach that same conclusion.)\n\n[ASSET: right | JarAndLockedCloset | width=14%] After the Human Top grabs Giant-Man's belt, he seems to know about his size-changing pills (which, if it were <i>me</i>, I would never advertise) -- and even though [LINK:1963:TTA_53_giant0|TTA #53|2027-04-02] showed the Porcupine grabbing capsules from Hank's belt (though ultimately grabbing the wrong ones and shrinking out of sight), at least the Human Top manages to guess <i>correctly</i> (there <i>is</i> a 50% chance of being right after all).\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut it seemed that Stan was basically out of ideas at that point.\n\nHaving grown to giant-size, he flings the still-normal-sized Hank into a nearby closet and [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=locks the door!]&nbsp; This notion has always struck me as very poor -- the idea of putting a lock with a key on a closet door, particularly one that cannot be opened from the inside!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=What a horrible design!]\n\nI get that from the <i>outside</i>, you might want to require a key so you can lock the door and prevent access, but what designer in his <i>right mind</i> would not provide an easy way to get out from the <i>inside</i> in the event that an older sibling (on a bad day) might lock his or her annoying kid brother in the closet!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]\n\nAnd Jan being trapped in a glass jar?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=Seriously?]&nbsp; Is that all it takes to capture her?&nbsp; Is she really helpless just because she's in a jar?&nbsp; I'm not sure that the text has ever suggested that she retains normal-sized strength when shrunken (the way Ant-Man does) [DECODER_RING: And I know she doesn't get strength enhancement until <i>way</i> later in <i>Marvel Team-Up</i> 59-60...], but if so, wouldn't even a normal sized human be able to crack a glass jar?\n\nFurthermore, does the Human Top actually believe that by capturing the Wasp and holding her hostage, Giant-Man will simply leave him <i>alone</i>?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=(Sure, that <b>totally</b> makes sense!)]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(What an idiot!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nNaturally, the ants come to Hank's rescue, leading to an ending that I felt far too convenient.\n\n[ASSET: left | WeakenedRoof | width=19%] The capture of the villain by weakening the rooftop by summoning <i>termites</i>?&nbsp; One look at the panel that shows how <b>thick</b> this roof actually was seems to make this <i>highly unlikely</i>, not to mention the <i>additional</i> unlikelihood of a building roof being constructed from [SPAN: severity=error; text=nothing but wood!]\n[BR]\n[BR] It's always nice to find out it was all done to the roof of a \"deserted tenement.\"\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(\"The old deserted building trick!\" -- <i>heh!</i>)]\n\n[ASSET: right | WeddingLicense | width=14%] And don't forget the gag-worthy ending, where Giant-Man not only provides details of their latest exploits to his fan club, but later, he reads that evening's newspaper headlines to Jan, who only continues to hold out hope that they will one day tie the knot.\n[BR]\n[BR] All of this only shows how lightweight their latest adventure actually was.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAs empty and devoid of calories as this month's story was, it was astonishingly... [SPAN: severity=info; text=passable.]\n\nThe Human Top as depicted here doesn't come across as terribly inventive, but the frightening fact is that he's at <i>least</i> more interesting than the other villains Giant-Man and the Wasp have faced and possesses a recognizable gimmick and a personality beyond \"scientist with a gadget,\" or \"criminal in a costume,\" or \"evil genius with exceptionally big noggin.\"\n\nThough I admit -- that doesn't actually say much for a title that I'm already on record as calling mostly dreadful.&nbsp; Despite the fact that it's been a <i>major</i> disappointment thus far, [SPAN: severity=success; text=let's at least give credit where it's due.]\n\nThis issue's plot was... [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=<i>okay</i>.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And the Human Top isn't especially <i>good</i>.&nbsp; He's just competing against a <i>remarkably</i> weak rogues' gallery.)]\n\nLet me be <i>very</i> clear though:\n[HANG] It won't set the world on fire...\n[HANG] It <i>most certainly</i> won't drive readers to clamor for reserve copies...\n[HANG] And though it had its share of absurdities, it... almost unbelievably... went down easy.\n\nI was never thrilled, but neither was I ever bored.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Is this actual <i>progress</i>?&nbsp; Or have my expectations just lowered?&nbsp; <i>Good question!</i>)]\n\nMy belief is that this title has been so devoid of life in recent months (with the exception of [LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-14], the Human Top's <i>first</i> appearance), that I have no choice but to conclude that I've been lulled into accepting as normal the otherwise lackluster adventures of Giant-Man and the Wasp.&nbsp; With that in mind, there is no way a story with this many distracting elements should be granted full reader status in the end.\n\n[HANG0] <i>Yes</i>, Stan, his first appearance <i>was</i> actually decent,\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=but this was a case of going to the well one too many times.]\n\nHaving the Human Top actually grasp the Hank/Jan relationship so quickly was [SPAN: severity=success; text=about the only noteworthy thing in the story.]&nbsp; In just a couple of minutes, the villain saw what Hank himself refuses to admit -- that the \"professional relationship\" of Giant-Man and the Wasp is anything <i>but</i>.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Most of the rest of the content amounted to... <i>uh...</i> spinning in place...]",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale:</b>  \"The Gypsy's Secret!\"\n[BR] (by Stan Lee, L. D. Lieber and George Bell)\n\nWhen the Wasp interrupts another of Hank's experiments, she declares that he wouldn't be satisfied even if he <i>found</i> what he was looking for, and to illustrate, she begins telling him a tale about an old gypsy named Gorko who uses alchemy in order to turn lead into gold.&nbsp; But even though it could transform his life, he chooses to live humbly and simply in his trailer, all the while searching for <i>something</i> -- in fact, the \"<i>only</i> thing of importance to me!\"\n\nBut one day, his wagon gets stuck in the mud on a road, and he's forced to request assistance at \"a nearby castle\" where a man named Baron Radzik lives.&nbsp; At first, he refuses to help the old man, and when he offers the Baron gold, he simply assumes the gypsy must have <i>stolen</i> it!\n\nBut when the gypsy indicates he can turn lead into gold, the Baron demands he reveal the technique -- the old man <i>refuses</i> to tell one whose heart is filled with such greed.&nbsp; Consequently, the Baron tosses the gypsy into his dungeon until such time as he reveals the secret!\n\nRelying on his wits, the gypsy lies and claims that the technique is too complicated to commit to memory and tells the Baron that he has it written down in his wagon home.&nbsp; The two <i>immediately</i> set out for the wagon.\n\nBut when the Baron enters and finds it dark, he is suddenly trapped by iron bars, and the wagon sheds its outer form and reveals that it is a spaceship, which rockets into the air.&nbsp; To his horror, the Baron learns that the gypsy is <i>really</i> an alien, who is from the \"ninth galaxy,\" sent to Earth to collect a human specimen for an \"intergalactic zoo.\"\n\nBaron Radzik is to be that specimen, for who on Earth would ever miss so greedy a person as he, or \"even shed a tear?\"&nbsp; The alien then assures him that he will indeed share his gold formula, but it will do him little good since gold is a very common metal on his world.\n\nAnd with the moral lesson told (that one can get what one wants and gain no benefit from it), the Wasp concludes.&nbsp; But she learns that her story has only given Hank a <i>new</i> idea:&nbsp; that of discovering the \"ancient formula for turning lead into gold!\"\n\nIt appears there will be no dinner and dancing tonight.&nbsp; She turns sadly away."
  ],
  "highlight": "The Human Top learns more about Hank and Jan's relationship in five minutes than Hank has in months."
}